    no: 0,
    kind: "reconstruction",
    field: "first cabalistic table, rows 1-56",
    printed: "",
    used: "",
    why: "Rows 62 to 72 are on the reissue's plate, and 57 to 72 on the 1823 printing's; 1 to 56 are on no image of Lenain's plate found. They are put back from each genius's own entry, which carries the same three columns, and are marked as reconstructed wherever they are shown — never written into the transcription, which is what the page prints. The method can be checked against the rows the plates do show, and the reconstructed table says how often it recovers each column; the misses are places where Lenain's table and his entries differ, such as Mogols for Mongols and Habuiah for Habuhiah. He names his own sources under the table — Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus, tome 2, pages 273 and 287, and Agrippa's Occult Philosophy, book 3, pages 109 and 110, The Hague 1727 — and both have been consulted. Agrippa gives the seventy-two names in the spellings Lenain uses, without nations or names of God. Kircher gives the seventy-two angels by choir in Hebrew and Latin (pp. 271 to 273), and on p. 287 introduces a plate of seventy-two four-letter names of God, each with its nation, which the table's last two columns follow row for row, from the Hebrews to Thrace. The plate is not in the copy those pages were read from (archive.org A027083); it was transcribed from the National Library of Poland's copy (Wikimedia Commons, \"Kircher-Diagram of the names of God (22593924).jpg\"), and the reconstructed table sets each of its rays beside the row. Rows 1 to 56 still rest on Lenain's entries, not on his plate, but each now stands beside its source, and where an entry departs from the plate a note on it says how and whose the difference is.",
  },
